# Example of an environment variables file. Copy to `.env` and fill in the values.
#
# ATTENTION. Vite inserts variables with the VITE_ prefix directly into the bundle.
# Everything that gets here will be available to anyone who opens the application sources.
# Secrets (private keys, mnemonics, API keys with the right signature) are here
# you CAN'T post it under any circumstances.

# The Alchemy API key is the default source of RPC nodes.
#
# THE WALLET WORKS WITHOUT A KEY: public nodes from the configuration are used
# networks. This is a working condition, not a bug.
#
# REQUIRED WHEN FILLING OUT. The key will be included in the bundle in plain text
# and will be visible to any user of the application. Limit it to the domain
# applications in the Alchemy panel (the "Allowlist" section of the application), otherwise the key
# will be used by outsiders and the quota will be exhausted in a matter of hours.
#
# ABOUT PRIVACY. One operator that handles all wallet requests sees
# The user's IP address and each address whose balance is being requested. This
# enough to link a personality to a briefcase. The user can specify
# own node in the network settings — then Alchemy is not used at all.
# VITE_ALCHEMY_API_KEY=

# Demo access key to CoinGecko — the source of courses for the portfolio.
#
# THE WALLET WORKS WITHOUT A KEY. Free public access takes one
# the address of the contract for the request (verified by contacting the service: failure 10012),
# therefore, courses are requested one at a time and slower. Addresses go away with the key
# in batches: fewer requests, fewer footprints, lower limit consumption.
#
# COURSES ARE NOT REQUESTED WITHOUT THE USER'S CONSENT. Having a key is nothing
# does not include: consent is asked on the briefcase screen with a listing
# of what exactly will come out.
#
# ABOUT PRIVACY. The token rate is requested at the address of its contract,
# in other words, the service learns the composition of the portfolio and the IP address. Wallet address
# not shared: the service does not know whose portfolio it is.
# VITE_COINGECKO_API_KEY=

# WalletConnect Project ID (Reown) — connecting applications.
#
# WITHOUT A KEY, THE SECTION OPENS and honestly reports that it is not configured;
# the library doesn't even load at the same time. The rest of the wallet is working.
#
# The key is public by the nature of the client application and does not grant signing rights:
# relay uses it to account for traffic. You can get it in the Reown panel.
#
# ABOUT PRIVACY. The Relay server sees your account addresses, metadata
 The # of each connected application and the time of each request. This is comparable
to a leak through the history indexer, so the connection is being performed
# by explicit user action, not by itself.
# VITE_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID=

# Simulation of transactions via Tenderly.
#
# ONLY FOR CHECKING ON YOUR OWN MACHINE. The access key gets into the text
 The # of the compiled program goes to everyone who opened the page: he gives
# the right to spend the quota of the project and read the history of its simulations.
# For the uploaded builds, the same data is entered in the wallet settings,
# where they are stored in encrypted storage and belong to the same owner.
#
# Without these values, the transaction consequence considers the node to be a method
# eth_simulateV1 is a working condition, not a limitation.
#
# Values are taken in Tenderly: Settings -> Authorization (key),
# project address of the type tenderly.co /<account>/<project>.
# VITE_TENDERLY_ACCOUNT=
# VITE_TENDERLY_PROJECT=
# VITE_TENDERLY_ACCESS_KEY=
